sentences.l:
for indeed he, marvelling, sat, at both war and fighting,
on high, on the topmost peak of wooded Samos,
the Thracian;

and around their feet fetters he cast, golden,
unbreakable, not to be undone, so that firmly on the spot they would stay
for their lord, having returned;

And the Trojans, equal to flame, in a close mass, or to a storm-blast,
after Hector, Priam’s-son, unceasingly eager, followed,
roaring, shouting;

“Ajax, since some one of the gods who hold Olympus
making himself like a seer bids us two fight beside the ships,
and he is not Calchas, the diviner, the bird-diviner;

“So now to me too about my spear the invincible hands
rage, and for me fury has been roused, and below with my feet
I am hurrying, with both;
